This line works, but I want to introduce additional delays for the new stroke-out and sweep animations I just added in the CSS. Those animations use the custom properties `--stroke-out-delay` and `--sweep-delay`, which currently have default values set via CSS variables (`var(--stroke-out-delay,2.4s)` and `var(--sweep-delay,1.55s)`). To make the timing coherent with the fill animation, I need to compute these delays based on the fill delay, rather than leaving them static.

I should calculate a base `fillDelay` first, then set `--fill-delay` as before. After that, I can set `--stroke-out-delay` to be slightly after the fill starts, say `fillDelay + 0.15s`, so the strokes fade out just as the fill becomes visible. Similarly, the sweep animation should start a bit later, maybe `fillDelay + 0.45s`, to give a nice trailing effect.

I must ensure the code remains clear and doesn't introduce redundant calculations. I'll store the computed fill delay in a variable `fillDelay` (a number, not a string), then set the CSS property using `fillDelay.toFixed(3) + 's'`. Then I’ll set the other two properties using the same pattern, adding the appropriate offsets.

I need to replace only the line that sets `--fill-delay`. The surrounding code likely looks like:

```js
